                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the learner and the failure curve`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before you design anything, understand <strong>why the existing certificates lose 39% of senior managers</strong>. The persona is time-poor, sceptical of “training”, motivated by peer status and immediate applicability — and the completion data shows a brutal cliff between weeks 3 and 5. Your job is to separate genuine <strong>engagement drivers</strong> — relevance, a sense of progress, social accountability — from <em>engagement theatre</em> like production gloss and optional extras that feel premium but move nothing. The trap: blaming the content, the video polish, or the learners, when the real failure is a <strong>design</strong> that loses momentum exactly when busy executives are deciding whether to stay.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose formats and the in-person / online blend`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `This is the core design decision. Allocate the <strong>€540,000</strong> across the format menu — a live in-person residential (richest engagement, but €95k plus €1.1k/learner travel), live online cohort sessions, a self-paced async backbone, and a social-accountability layer of peer circles and milestones. The wish lists sum to €880,000, so you <em>cannot</em> buy everything. Two residentials nearly eat the whole envelope; going fully self-paced to protect cost-per-learner drags completion back toward the 48% async floor the board explicitly rejected. Find the blend that maximises engagement and outcomes <strong>within budget</strong>.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence the assessments and engineer momentum`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Now sequence learning and assessment across the 10 weeks to counter the cliff. Decide the rhythm of <strong>formative checks</strong> (€1.8k each, feedback-rich, your early-warning system), where to place them, whether to add the sponsor-visible <strong>capstone</strong> (€22k), and how firmly to build a <strong>backward-design outcome-evidence trail</strong> for EQUIS. Reconcile the three factions — faculty's single final, learning science's frequent low-stakes checks, sponsors' visible capstone — into one defensible architecture. The trap: spreading checks <em>evenly</em> across all 10 weeks defends week 9, where retention is fine, as hard as week 4, where you bleed learners. Design follows the curve, not the calendar.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Pilot results and iterate`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The pilot telemetry is in, and it missed your projection. Some teams over-invested in residentials and blew cost-per-learner; others under-engineered momentum and watched the week-4 cliff reappear. <strong>Diagnose the gap</strong> honestly — was it a design fault or “bad luck”? — then reallocate for the full 120-seat v2 under the same €540,000. And the sting: hitting completion is not enough. The Vice-Dean ties your bonus to <em>outcomes</em> precisely to stop you shipping a course everyone finishes and no one learns from. Lift completion <strong>and</strong> outcome attainment together — they are one joint objective, not a trade-off.`,

                    r1_root_label:          `Why do the existing certificates lose 39% of senior managers?`,
                    r1_lever_label:         `Which lever actually moves engagement (vs merely feeling premium)?`,
                    r1_focus_label:         `How sharply do you concentrate the diagnosis on the week 3–5 cliff? (%)`,
                    r1_focus_readout:       `Diagnostic focus on the diagnosed drop-off window:`,
                    root_design:            `A design problem — courses feel like lectures, lose momentum, and have no social accountability by week 4`,
                    root_content:           `A content problem — the material simply isn't good or deep enough yet`,
                    root_production:        `A production problem — the videos and slides aren't polished and premium enough`,
                    root_learners:          `The learners — busy executives who were never that committed in the first place`,
                    lever_drivers:          `Engagement drivers — relevance, a sense of progress, and social accountability`,
                    lever_gloss:            `Production gloss — broadcast-quality video and a beautiful learning platform`,
                    lever_extras:           `Optional extras — a rich library of bonus readings and webinars to choose from`,
                    lever_none:             `Nothing reliable — engagement is mostly down to the individual learner`,

                    r2_inperson_label:      `Live in-person residential (€95k fixed + €1.1k/learner travel · richest engagement, lowest scale)`,
