                    coach_default:          `Design decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> It's not the content or the video polish — it's a design that loses momentum by week 4. Busy executives don't quit because the material is thin; they quit when nothing pulls them back: no relevance to today's problem, no sense of progress, no peer who'll notice they've gone quiet.`,
                    coach_r1_production:    `<strong>You're reaching for production gloss.</strong> Broadcast-quality video feels premium, but your own data shows the cliff is weeks 3–5, not “the slides weren't shiny enough.” Gloss is engagement <em>theatre</em>. The drivers are relevance, momentum and social accountability — design those, not the polish.`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>Careful — this isn't a content gap.</strong> The faculty know the material; that's not why a senior manager goes quiet in week 4. Adding more depth to a course nobody is staying in just makes a richer thing people still abandon. Fix the journey first.`,
                    coach_r1_learners:      `<strong>Blaming the learners is the comfortable answer.</strong> When 39% of every cohort drops at the same point, that's a design signal, not a character flaw. Time-poor executives stay when the course earns their next hour — that's your job to engineer, not theirs to supply.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a design problem AND identified the real levers — relevance, progress and social accountability — over the theatre of gloss and extras. That's exactly the learner-experience lens that lets you design the journey rather than decorate it.`,
                    coach_r1_gloss:         `<strong>You've bet on the wrong lever.</strong> Production gloss photographs well in a board deck and changes nothing on the week-4 cliff. The euros that move completion buy relevance, momentum and accountability — not a prettier player.`,
                    coach_r1_levernone:     `<strong>“It's down to the learner” is a non-diagnosis.</strong> If engagement were purely individual you couldn't predict the cliff — but you can, every cohort, weeks 3–5. That predictability is the design lever. Use it.`,
                    coach_r1_lowfocus:      `<strong>You've spread your attention thin.</strong> The risk is concentrated in weeks 3–5; a diagnosis that doesn't zoom in there will produce a design that defends everywhere and nowhere. Point the lens at the cliff.`,

                    coach_r2_resone:        `<strong>One anchoring residential is a smart bet.</strong> A single in-person kick-off builds the relationships and accountability that carry the cohort through the online weeks — the richest engagement euro, spent once, where it sets up everything after.`,
                    coach_r2_restwo:        `<strong>Two residentials are gorgeous — and nearly €454k of your €540k.</strong> The engagement is real, but you've left almost nothing for assessment, social accountability or iteration. Is the second residential worth more than the entire momentum design it crowds out?`,
                    coach_r2_resnone:       `<strong>Going fully remote protects cost-per-learner</strong> — but you've given up the single richest relationship-builder. With no in-person anchor, your social-accountability layer has to work twice as hard. Is it funded to?`,
                    coach_r2_async:         `<strong>A fully self-paced build is the cheapest-blend fallacy.</strong> Your own data says self-paced certificates for senior managers complete at 48%, and the board wants 85%. You've optimised the one number — cost — that nobody on the board is asking about. Where is the live rhythm that keeps a busy executive from quietly dropping in week 4?`,
                    coach_r2_social:        `<strong>Funding the social-accountability layer is high-leverage.</strong> Peer circles and visible milestones are the cheapest engagement euros you'll spend — for time-poor executives, a peer who notices you've gone quiet outperforms production polish many times over.`,
                    coach_r2_nosocial:      `<strong>You've under-funded social accountability.</strong> Relatedness — a cohort that notices you — is exactly what stops the week-4 quiet exit. Skimping here is skimping on the one lever that's cheap and works.`,
                    coach_r2_cheapblend:    `<strong>Fully async and almost no social layer — the cliff is now a cliff edge.</strong> This is the exact blend your 48% history predicts. You've protected cost-per-learner and surrendered the board's 85% target in the same decision.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Your blueprint is gorgeous — and it costs more than €540,000.</strong> Your contribution margin is 31%; an over-budget design turns a profitable flagship into a loss. The Dean sold this as proof the Studio designs smart, not lavish. Which delight are you cutting — and why didn't you start from the constraint?`,

                    coach_r3_cliff:         `<strong>Concentrating density at the cliff is design following the curve.</strong> Putting your formative checks and momentum interventions in weeks 3–5 means you catch a struggling learner in week 4 — when you can re-engage them — not in week 9 when they fail.`,
                    coach_r3_even:          `<strong>Even spacing ignores your own diagnosis.</strong> One check per week defends week 9, where retention is already fine, as hard as week 4, where you bleed learners. Same effort, wrong target. Where is the intervention density highest?`,
                    coach_r3_late:          `<strong>Back-loading assessment misses the whole point.</strong> The cliff is weeks 3–5; checks clustered near the end tell you a learner is lost long after you could have saved them. Move the rhythm forward, onto the curve.`,
                    coach_r3_architecture:  `<strong>That's a defensible assessment architecture.</strong> Formative rhythm at the cliff for early-warning and momentum, a visible capstone for the sponsors and outcome evidence — you've reconciled the three factions without averaging them into mush.`,
                    coach_r3_finalonly:     `<strong>One high-stakes final, and nothing before it.</strong> But your cliff is weeks 3–5, long before any exam. With no formative checkpoints you find out a learner is lost in week 9 when they fail, not week 4 when you could have re-engaged them — and a single terminal exam is thin outcome evidence for EQUIS.`,
                    coach_r3_nocapstone:    `<strong>No capstone saves €22k but costs you the sponsor's showcase.</strong> Corporate sponsors want a visible capstone their executives can show their own boards — and it's strong outcome evidence for the accreditation panel. Cutting it is a false economy.`,
                    coach_r3_noevidence:    `<strong>Thin outcome-evidence trail.</strong> Backward design starts from the outcomes you must prove. If your assessments don't visibly evidence stated learning outcomes, the EQUIS panel sees activity, not attainment — and the Vice-Dean's bonus policy has nothing to reward.`,

                    coach_r4_designmiss:    `<strong>Owning it as a design fault is where iteration starts.</strong> Your week-4 engagement telemetry collapsed exactly where your diagnosis predicted and you placed nothing there. That's not noise — that's a miss you can fix. Now reallocate toward the curve.`,
                    coach_r4_badluck:       `<strong>“The cohort was unusually busy” is a rationalisation.</strong> Maybe — but the data collapsed precisely where you predicted the cliff and left it undefended. Iteration starts with owning the design miss, not explaining the number away.`,
                    coach_r4_learners:      `<strong>Blaming the learners again — twice now.</strong> Two cohorts, same drop-off point, is a design pattern, not a run of bad luck with people. The data is telling you something your blueprint ignored. Listen to it.`,
                    coach_r4_curve:         `<strong>Reallocating toward the diagnosed risk is exactly right.</strong> Move euros from premium gloss to momentum and accountability at the cliff. You're not buying more delight — you're buying it where the curve says it counts.`,
                    coach_r4_residential:   `<strong>“More in-person always lifts engagement” is the wish-list reflex returning.</strong> Another residential adds €227k you don't have and treats the symptom, not the week-4 design gap. Engagement isn't bought by the most expensive format; it's engineered at the point of risk.`,
                    coach_r4_flat:          `<strong>Keeping v1 unchanged is not iteration.</strong> The pilot told you where it broke; leaving the design flat repeats the miss at 120 seats and €540,000. Data-driven design means changing the thing the data indicted.`,
                    coach_r4_joint:         `<strong>That's the central discipline.</strong> Applied tasks tied to real work lift outcome attainment <em>and</em> engagement at once — the busy executive stays because it's useful and learns because it's applied. Engagement and outcomes as one objective, exactly why your bonus is tied to outcomes.`,
                    coach_r4_completion:    `<strong>You hit completion by making it easy — and outcomes slid.</strong> A course everyone finishes and no one learns from is a more expensive failure than one they quit. The Vice-Dean ties your bonus to outcomes precisely to stop this. How do you lift attainment without re-triggering the drop-off?`,
                    coach_r4_rigour:        `<strong>Cranking up rigour protects outcomes but re-triggers the cliff.</strong> A harder final on a course people are already abandoning just makes them abandon it sooner. Outcomes and engagement move together — you can't buy one by sacrificing the other.`,

